    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0796" n="710" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg710"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Riddle to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1903-06-15"><hi rendition="#italic">June 15,
                        1903</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Riddle reports that the Russian minister for foreign affairs requests that
                    the minister of the United States to China communicate to the Russian minister
                    to China the demands of the United States <pb facs="0797" n="711" xml:id="pg711"
                    /> relative to localities to be opened to trade, and promises that the minister
                    of Russia will be authorized to reply frankly to the minister of the United
                    States and the Chinese Government what the attitude of the Russian Government
                    is. The minister for foreign affairs states to Mr. Riddle that because of the
                    distance, and without knowing what localities are meant, he can not blindly
                    commit himself, as he admits there are some ports whose opening Russia would not
                    favor before the Russian evacuation of Manchuria under satisfactory guaranties
                    is completed.</p>
                <p>A frank interchange of views between the two ministers at Peking is recommended
                    by the minister for foreign affairs.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
